---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Single sign-on">
  <h1>Single sign-on</h1>
  <p>
    When one deployment serves several products, a user who signed into one of
    them should not be sent through the whole provider dance again to open the
    next. SSO gives identity a memory of <em>this browser already
    authenticated</em>, so a second product can be served immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>SSO shares an authentication, never a token pair.</strong> Each
    product still receives its own access + refresh pair, minted fresh, with
    its own rotation lineage. Nothing is handed between products — which is
    what keeps a compromise of the weakest product from becoming a compromise
    of all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      A user completes a hosted sign-in at <code>/oauth/callback/{"{provider}"}</code>.
      Alongside the usual one-time code, identity sets
      <code>__Host-sso_session</code> — an opaque, <code>HttpOnly</code>,
      <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code> cookie locked to the auth
      origin. Only its SHA-256 is stored; the cookie is never sent to a product
      origin.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your sign-in page asks <code>GET /sso/session</code> who the browser
      belongs to, and renders <strong>"Continue as &lt;email&gt;"</strong> next
      to <strong>"Use a different account"</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Choosing continue navigates to <code>/oauth/continue</code>, which mints
      the same single-use <code>?code=</code> a completed provider round trip
      produces and redirects to <code>return_to</code>. Your app redeems it
      with the unchanged <code>RedeemOAuthCode</code> RPC.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Because the fast path ends at the one-time code, every property of token
    issuance is inherited rather than re-implemented: per-product pairs,
    rotation, session records, and product age gating all happen on the normal
    redeem path.
  </p>

  <h2>Skipping the provider skips nothing else</h2>
  <p>
    Having authenticated is not authorization to enter a given product. Before
    a code is minted, <code>/oauth/continue</code> re-runs the checks a cold
    sign-in runs: the <code>return_to</code> allowlist, account status
    (lockout, deactivation, identity verification), the project's access mode,
    and the tenant's login policy — the last replayed against the login method
    that <em>established</em> the session, so a cookie can never launder a weak
    login into a stronger one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Accounts owing a second factor get no fast path. A challenge cannot be
    completed inside a redirect, and a months-old cookie must not stand in for
    the factor; those users take the full sign-in flow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sessions are scoped to their project. A session established in one project
    is invisible to another, so a consumer sign-in can never fast-path into a
    separate, allowlisted admin project.
  </p>

  <h2>Sign-out</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Call</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>Logout</code></td>
        <td>
          That product's tokens only. <strong>The SSO session is
          untouched</strong> — signing out of one app is not signing out of the
          account, and the next product still offers Continue as.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SignOutEverywhere</code> / <code>RevokeAllSessions</code></td>
        <td>
          Every refresh token, every access session, <strong>and every SSO
          session</strong> for the user. This is the one call that ends the
          browser's ability to keep minting new product sessions.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ChangePassword</code></td>
        <td>The same full revocation — a credential change forces re-auth everywhere.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    SSO is <strong>off by default</strong>. Enabling it also requires
    <code>GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS</code>, which
    <code>/oauth/continue</code> validates exactly as <code>/oauth/start</code>
    does.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`GATEWAY_SSO_ENABLED=true

# Rolling lifetime — re-anchored on every use. Default 90 days.
GATEWAY_SSO_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=7776000

# tap (default) shows a card naming the account before minting.
# silent forwards with no interstitial.
GATEWAY_SSO_CONTINUE_MODE=tap

# Exact origins of your sign-in page, allowed to read GET /sso/session
# with credentials. Empty (default) leaves that endpoint unregistered.
GATEWAY_SSO_HUB_ORIGINS=https://accounts.example.com`}
  />

  <h3>Why <code>tap</code> is the default</h3>
  <p>
    The failure modes are not symmetric. Silent SSO on a shared or family
    device signs a second person in as the first, invisibly, and no product
    downstream can tell it happened. One tap costs a fraction of a second and
    puts the account on screen before anything is minted. Set
    <code>silent</code> only where every device has exactly one user.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the session from your sign-in page</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /sso/session</code> answers identically for "no cookie" and "dead
    cookie", and returns no account data in the negative case. It must be
    called with credentials, from an origin on
    <code>GATEWAY_SSO_HUB_ORIGINS</code>.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="js"
    code={`const res = await fetch("https://auth.example.com/sso/session", {
  credentials: "include",
});
const { authenticated, email, continueMode } = await res.json();

if (authenticated) {
  const target =
    "https://auth.example.com/oauth/continue" +
    "?return_to=" + encodeURIComponent(RETURN_TO) +
    "&fallback_to=" + encodeURIComponent(SIGN_IN_URL);

  if (continueMode === "silent") {
    window.location.assign(target);   // no interstitial
  } else {
    showContinueAsCard(email, target); // one visible tap
  }
}`}
  />
  <p>
    Pass <code>fallback_to</code> (allowlisted like <code>return_to</code>) so
    a session that lapsed between the card and the tap returns the user to your
    sign-in page with <code>?session=expired</code> instead of an error. A real
    denial — a refused age gate, an off-allowlist account, a deactivated
    user — answers <code>403</code> rather than bouncing back, so the browser
    cannot loop against a door that will keep saying no.
  </p>

  <h2>Native apps</h2>
  <p>
    Apps that route sign-in through the system browser
    (<code>ASWebAuthenticationSession</code>, Chrome Custom Tabs) get SSO with
    no code change, because the cookie lands in the shared browser jar. An app
    that requests an <em>ephemeral</em> session opts itself out — on iOS that
    is <code>prefersEphemeralWebBrowserSession = true</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
